.TH SSHW 1 "November 2026" "winless 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sshw \- ssh, with winless working on the remote host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sshw
[\fIssh-options\fR] \fIhost\fR [\fIcommand\fR]
.br
.B winless ssh
[\fIssh-options\fR] \fIhost\fR [\fIcommand\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshw
is
.BR ssh (1)
for a person who also uses
.BR winless (1).
It gives you the shell (or runs the command) you would get from ssh, and inside it
.B winless open FILE
shows the file in a window on your desktop, while the editing, writing, history, git commits, filters and model revisions all happen
on the host, next to the file. Nothing needs setting up by hand: the tunnels, the servers and the window are started when needed.
.PP
Every option and argument is passed to ssh as you typed it (\-p, \-i, \-J, \-o, user@host, a command ...), so
.B alias ssh=sshw
works for interactive use.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sshw myserver
.fi
.RS
A login shell on myserver. Inside it,
.B winless open notes.txt
shows notes.txt in a window on your desktop, and
.BR "winless goto 120" ,
.B winless search ...
and the other commands drive that window from the host's shell. Leaving the shell closes the connection.
.RE
.PP
.nf
sshw \-p 2222 \-i ~/.ssh/work \-J bastion alice@build01
sshw \-A myserver
.fi
.RS
Ordinary ssh options (\-A, \-X, \-Y, \-a, \-x, \-t, \-T and \-e apply to the shell; the rest to the connection).
.RE
.PP
.nf
sshw myserver 'winless open \-\-wait \-N /var/log/syslog'
.fi
.RS
Run one command. \fB\-\-wait\fR keeps the session open until the window is quit; without it the command returns as soon as the window
is up, which ends the session, and the window then has nothing behind it.
.RE
.PP
.nf
alias ssh=sshw
.fi
.RS
For people who never want to think about it.
.RE
.SH HOW IT WORKS
.B sshw
opens one ssh connection that stays up for the session (ssh's ControlMaster; you authenticate once, as usual, and your ~/.ssh/config
applies). Through it, a private unix socket is forwarded from the host back to a small relay process on your desktop, and the host's
shell gets $WINLESS_BELL naming that socket.
.PP
On the host,
.B winless open
starts its server beside the file as it always does. Having no screen, it then rings the bell: the relay forwards the server's port to
your desktop (with
.BR "ssh \-O forward" ,
through the same connection, so no further login) and opens the window there. The window is opened on an address that carries the
server's secret, which the server trades for a cookie; the forward is removed when the server goes away. Closing the shell ends the
connection and the relay.
.SH HOSTS WITHOUT WINLESS
On connecting,
.B sshw
asks the host what it is (uname \-sm) and whether a login shell there already finds a winless that knows about this (winless \-\-features
prints "bell"). If not, it uploads its own: a static binary built for that platform, and viewer.html, into
~/.cache/winless/remote/HASH/ on the host (once; later sessions find them there), and puts that directory first on the PATH of the
shell it starts. The bytes travel through an ssh exec channel without a terminal, which is 8-bit clean, and the uploaded program is run
once on the host to prove it works there.
.PP
The binaries live in an archive with one entry per platform, named as uname \-sm prints it with the space made a hyphen
(Linux\-x86_64, Linux\-aarch64, Darwin\-arm64):
~/.local/share/winless/remote/OS\-MACHINE/winless, and the copy that
.B make install
puts under the data directory.
.B make
builds the static binary for the machine it runs on (it needs libc.a and libsqlite3.a) as remote/OS\-MACHINE/winless.
.PP
For any other platform the archive fills itself. If the host has no binary in the archive but does have a C compiler (cc, gcc or clang) with
the sqlite3 headers and the static libc and sqlite libraries, sshw sends it the sources (winless\-src.tar.gz, made by make and installed beside
viewer.html), builds a static winless there, copies the result back into the archive, and uses it. The next host of that kind is just an upload.
A binary can also be placed in the archive by hand (built on such a machine, or cross-built). If there is none and none can be built, the
shell still starts; only winless is missing on the host, and sshw says so.
.SH OPTIONS
All of ssh's options are accepted. Those that describe the shell session itself (\-A \-a \-X \-x \-Y \-T \-t \-e) are given to the session; all
others (\-p \-i \-J \-o \-L ...) to the connection that sshw keeps up. As with ssh, a login shell gets a terminal and a command does not,
unless \-t is given (\-T never).
.PP
Options that would interfere with the connection that sshw manages (\-M \-S \-N \-f \-O \-W) are refused.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
On the desktop: OpenSSH 6.7 or later (for forwarding a unix socket), and the browser winless uses. On the host: sshd allowing forwarding
(AllowTcpForwarding and AllowStreamLocalForwarding, both on by default), and a POSIX-like login shell (bash, zsh, sh; not fish or csh).
.SH EXIT STATUS
That of the remote shell or command, as with ssh. 1 if the connection or the setup failed, 2 if sshw was invoked incorrectly.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B winless: no window can be opened from this ssh session; reconnect with `winless ssh HOST'
winless was run in a plain ssh session, not one started by sshw.
.TP
.B winless: the ssh session's window helper did not answer
The sshw that started the shell has ended (or its relay stopped); see relay.log in the session directory.
.TP
.B the desktop could not open the window: ssh could not forward the port
sshd on the host refuses forwarding (see REQUIREMENTS).
.TP
.B the desktop could not open the window: no browser found on the desktop
Set WINLESS_BROWSER on the desktop.
.TP
.B winless ssh: HOST has no winless and I have none built for OS MACHINE
See HOSTS WITHOUT WINLESS: place a static build under ~/.local/share/winless/remote/. The shell still starts, without winless.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WINLESS_SSH
The ssh program to run (default: ssh).
.TP
.B WINLESS_REMOTE_DIR
Use this directory as the archive of binaries (DIR/OS\-MACHINE/winless) instead of the default places; new builds are kept there too.
.TP
.B WINLESS_BELL
Set by sshw in the host's shell: the socket the host's winless rings to ask your desktop for a window.
.TP
.B WINLESS_BROWSER
The browser used for the window (on your desktop), as for winless.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/winless/ID/
The session's control socket, bell socket and relay log (or /tmp/winless\-UID/ID/); removed when the session ends.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/winless/remote/HASH/
On the host: the uploaded winless and viewer.html.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/winless/ports/PORT
On the host: a server's process id and its secret, readable by you only.
.SH SECURITY
Each winless server holds a random secret and refuses every request (except /ping) that does not carry it: the command line reads it from
a file only you can read, and a window is given it in the address it is opened with. Other users of the same host cannot drive your servers
(which can run filters and write files), nor can web pages, which are also refused by Host and Origin.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR winless (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.SH AUTHOR
David Walther.
